{"id": "unknown-2026-10-14-035", "date": "2026-10-14", "time_tags": {"year": 2026, "quarter": "Q4", "month": 10, "week": 42, "weekday": "Wed"}, "entities": {"companies": [], "customers": [], "suppliers": []}, "topics": ["industry_general"], "sentiment": {"label": "neutral", "score": 0.0, "keywords": []}, "importance": {"score": 0.5, "reasons": []}, "title": "Study reveals up to 15% gap between expected and actual bifacial gains in utility-scale PV", "content": "<p>Researchers in Spain have found that the effective bifaciality of solar modules in utility-scale PV plants can be up to 15% lower than their nominal rating. Based on two years of operational data from a 50 MWp solar plant in Spain, with findings validated across four additional installations in Spain and the United Kingdom, the study highlights the influence of irradiance, temperature, mounting geometry and operating conditions on rear-side power generation.</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://w", "sources": [{"name": "pv_magazine", "url": "https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/10/14/study-reveals-up-to-15-gap-between-expected-and-actual-bifacial-gains-in-utility-scale-pv/", "type": "rss"}]}
